# cliProxy Rules

- Treat this project as `cliProxy`, an OpenAI-compatible proxy for CLI model clients such as Codex CLI, Copilot CLI, and other OpenAI-compatible command-line clients.
- Keep the public API base compatible with OpenAI-style clients at `https://<host>/v1`.
- Keep both primary API surfaces working:
  - Codex CLI uses `POST /v1/responses`.
  - Copilot CLI and Chat Completions clients use `POST /v1/chat/completions`.
- Keep `GET /v1/models` returning configured model IDs from `CLIPROXY_MODELS`.
- `/v1/responses` must support all routed providers. Azure OpenAI uses native upstream Responses; DeepSeek, Kimi, MiniMax, and Azure Anthropic use the internal Responses bridge.
- Use `CLIPROXY_API_KEY` for client auth and `CLIPROXY_MODELS` for model discovery.
- Advertise and document model IDs as either bare IDs, such as `deepseek-v4-pro`, or `cliproxy/<model>`.
- Legacy names (`VSCODEPROXY_*`, `vscodeproxy/<model>`, and `azure/<model>`) are accepted only for migration compatibility. Do not use them in new examples except explicit migration notes.
- Do not reintroduce old project branding, old primary env var names, old model prefixes, or VS Code/Cursor-specific positioning in product docs or examples.
- Provider keys must stay server-side: `DEEPSEEK_API_KEY`, `KIMI_API_KEY`, `MINIMAX_API_KEY`, and `AZURE_FOUNDRY_API_KEY`.
- Docker/local Node, Vercel Edge, and EdgeOne Pages Cloud Functions must continue to route through the shared `api/proxy.js` handler.

## Responses Bridge Limitations (reference before investigating)

The Responses bridge (`api/responses-bridge.js`) converts `/v1/responses` to Chat Completions for DeepSeek, Kimi, MiniMax, and Azure Anthropic. It is inherently lossy — Chat Completions has no concept of built-in tool execution or server-side state.

### Tool type handling

| Tool type | Bridge behavior |
|---|---|
| `function` | Converted to Chat Completions function tools |
| `custom` (e.g. `apply_patch`) | Converted to Chat Completions function tools |
| `namespace` | Recursively flattened; child functions extracted |
| `web_search`, `web_search_preview` | Silently skipped — no CC equivalent |
| `code_interpreter` | Silently skipped — no CC equivalent |
| `file_search` | Silently skipped — no CC equivalent |
| `computer_use_preview`, `computer_use` | Silently skipped — no CC equivalent |
| `shell`, `local_shell` | Silently skipped — no CC equivalent |
| `image_generation` | Silently skipped — no CC equivalent |
| `mcp` | Silently skipped — MCP execution is client-side |
| Any unknown built-in | Silently skipped |

### Tool call transcript repair

`normalizeChatToolSequence()` in `api/responses-bridge.js` repairs malformed tool-call history:
- **Merges** consecutive `function_call` items into a single `assistant` message with array `tool_calls` (Chat Completions requires this; Responses sends them as separate items).
- **Merges** a preceding `output_text` into the same assistant message when tool calls follow.
- **Moves** delayed tool outputs next to the assistant tool call that invoked them.
- **Injects** placeholder results for tool calls whose outputs are missing from the transcript.
- **Drops** orphan tool results with no matching call.

Diagnostics: `TOOL_SEQUENCE_REPAIRED insertedMissing:N moved:N droppedInvalidCalls:N droppedOrphanResults:N`

### Known non-proxy causes for "unsupported call" errors

| Symptom | Root cause | Not a proxy bug |
|---|---|---|
| "unsupported call" for `search_web`, `get_repo_info`, `notion_search`, etc. | MCP server not connected or sandbox blocks network | Codex CLI MCP wiring / network config |
| "unsupported call" for `web_search`, `code_interpreter` | Chat Completions has no built-in tool execution | Architectural — the model can describe a search but the client must execute it |
| `apply_patch` returns "unsupported call" | Codex CLI sandbox/approval policy blocks it | Client-side; the tool definition is correctly forwarded by the proxy |

### Client-to-provider pairing guidance

- **Copilot CLI** (`/v1/chat/completions`) + any provider = works directly, no bridge needed.
- **Codex CLI** (`/v1/responses`) + Azure OpenAI = native Responses path, all tool types preserved.
- **Codex CLI** + DeepSeek/Kimi/MiniMax = bridge path, only function/custom tools work. Web search, MCP tools, and built-in execution are unavailable.

When a user reports Codex CLI tool failures against non-Azure providers, check the tool type first. If it matches a known limitation above, that may be the cause — but if the API or client has evolved, investigate freely.

## EdgeOne Observability

- Every deployment target's entry point must log REQ and RES lines with method, path, status, and wall-clock duration. Kubernetes/deployment-level metrics are not a substitute for in-function logs visible in the platform's native log viewer.
- Optional dependencies (KV, caching, features gated by env vars) must emit a single one-shot diagnostic at startup/init reporting whether they are available. A missing KV binding must never fail silently.
- Platform compatibility shims must log what they found (e.g. registered binding name) and what failed (e.g. which binding names were tried). The `setupEdgeOneCompatibility()` pattern applies: register, then report.
- Never leave logging/error helpers defined but unused. If a wrapper file defines `xxxLog` / `xxxError`, they must be wired into the actual request flow. Unused helpers signal intent that was never followed through.
- Silent failure paths are worse than noisy ones. Operators cannot diagnose what they cannot see. If a component can be degraded (KV unavailable, timeout shortened, feature disabled), log it explicitly.
- EdgeOne Pages Log Analysis only shows `console.log` / `console.error` output from Cloud Functions, not Edge Functions. All EdgeOne entry points live under `cloud-functions/` for this reason.

## Wiki Handling

- Treat `wiki/` as a checked-out GitHub Wiki repository, not as a normal source subfolder.
- The root repo intentionally ignores `wiki/`; root commits must not include wiki content.
- The wiki remote is `git@github.com:lqdflying/cliProxy.wiki.git`. Confirm with `git -C wiki remote -v` if unsure.
- Use root Git commands for main-code changes and `git -C wiki ...` for wiki changes. Never assume root `git status` includes wiki edits.
- Before editing wiki pages, run `git -C wiki status --short --branch` and preserve any user changes.
- After editing wiki pages, run `git -C wiki diff --check` and review `git -C wiki status --short --branch`.
- Commit and push wiki changes from inside the wiki repo only, or with `git -C wiki ...`, and only when the user asks to commit or push.
- Do not create a replacement `doc/wiki/`, `wiki/` copy, or generated wiki folder in the main repo.
- Keep wiki links aligned with actual wiki filenames. Current pages are `Home.md`, `Client-Setup.md`, `Deployment.md`, `Configuration.md`, `Architecture.md`, `Known-Issues.md`, and `Development.md`.
- When changing user-facing behavior, update both tracked docs (`README.md` / `doc/*.md`) and the GitHub Wiki when the change affects setup, configuration, deployment, routing, or known limitations.

## Validation

- Before committing JavaScript changes, run `node --check` on changed JS files and `git diff --check`.
- Before finishing a documentation-only wiki change, run `git -C wiki diff --check` and scan for stale product terms.
